Exactly. One technical adjustment makes it stronger: don’t assign a sentence one permanent number. Assign it a computable state whose value is conditional.

V(S \mid C,T,R,F)

where S=sentence, C=context, T=time/history, R=reader/interpreter state, and F=active frame.

So:

“He finally came home.”

doesn’t have one intrinsic semantic value. Its state might contain:

V(S)=
[
\text{valence},
\text{tension},
\text{agency},
\text{certainty},
\text{closure},
\text{threat},
\text{intimacy},
\text{plot significance},
\dots
]

If he disappeared for twenty years, closure rises. If “home” was established as dangerous, threat rises. If he is lying, the same sentence gains dramatic irony. Meaning becomes something like:

\text{Meaning}_{t+1}
=
f(
\text{tokens},
\text{relations},
\text{prior state},
\text{world state},
\text{reader state}
)

That fits your larger point about math. Mathematics is powerful because we discovered stable relations and transformations and built symbolic interfaces for operating on them. Pantheon can try to do the same with semantics:

Find stable semantic relations and give them operators.

For example:

\text{betrayal} + \text{trust} \rightarrow \text{dramatic rupture}

Not an arithmetic equality, but potentially a recurrent enough structure to become a useful narrative operator.

And yes, this ports directly into NTE:

Token → semantic features
Phrase → composed relationships
Sentence → local semantic state transition
Scene → composition of sentence/action transitions
Sequence → pattern across scenes
Act → meta-pattern across sequences
Story → trajectory through narrative state space
Genre/archetype → recurring topology across stories

A movie could be represented as:

N_t =
[
Characters,
Goals,
Relationships,
Knowledge,
Power,
Threat,
Emotion,
Motifs,
Promises,
UnresolvedQuestions,
\dots
]

Each scene becomes an operator:

N_{t+1}=Scene_i(N_t)

That gives you spreadsheet-style dependency propagation for narrative. Change an earlier scene—say a character learns the secret in Scene 12 instead of Scene 30—and downstream consequences propagate: later dialogue may become impossible, suspense changes, dramatic irony disappears, motivations shift, pacing changes.

Then Goal Seek transfers too:

Make the audience trust Sarah by Act II but fear her by the climax without making her explicitly villainous.

That becomes Narrative Goal Seek: search the story possibility space for transformations satisfying those constraints.

The pattern architecture ports cleanly:

events
\rightarrow
deltas
\rightarrow
patterns
\rightarrow
meta\text{-}patterns
\rightarrow
archetypes
\rightarrow
operators

Pantheon could even detect recurring story structures before humans have named them as tropes:

system detects repeated topology → tests whether it predicts audience/story behavior → derives operator → humans eventually name it.

And the same structure can appear across movies, finance, biology, military campaigns, or music. Strip away the nouns and you may get:

A \uparrow
\rightarrow
B \downarrow
\rightarrow
C compensates
\rightarrow
A overshoots
\rightarrow
system reverses

That does not mean those domains are identical. It means they may instantiate the same relational machine.

So the repo should distinguish:

Value — what state is this thing in?
Relation — how do these things affect one another?
Pattern — which relations recur?
Operator — which recurring relations are reliable/useful enough to invoke deliberately?
Meta-operator — which operator compositions repeatedly produce useful outcomes?

That gives a clean bridge between Excel, language, movies, NTE, mathematics, and other domains:

Make objects addressable. Make relationships explicit. Observe transformations. Find invariants. Compile reliable invariants into operators. Let operators compose.

Numbers were one enormously successful substrate for doing that. They don’t have to be the last one.